(version 1)

# SOLAR-GLOW DRH v4.0 — DUAL-FAB ENVELOPE (PCBWay ∩ OSH Park)
#
# Retargeted 2026-07-27. These floors are no longer "PCBWay with margin"; they are the
# INTERSECTION of two fabs, so one board file can be ordered from either without re-checking
# anything. The point is interchangeability: PCBWay for fast, cheap, local prototypes and small
# batches; OSH Park After Dark (black FR4 + clear mask) for the naked "midnight" variant.
#
#   parameter        PCBWay      OSH Park     governs      gate here
#   ---------------  ----------  -----------  -----------  ---------
#   trace width      0.100 mm    0.1524 mm    OSH Park     0.152 mm
#   clearance        0.100 mm    0.1524 mm    OSH Park     0.152 mm
#   drill            0.200 mm    0.254 mm     OSH Park     0.254 mm
#   annular ring     0.150 mm    0.127 mm     >> PCBWay << 0.1499 mm
#   copper -> edge   (n/s)       0.381 mm     OSH Park     0.381 mm
#
# Note the inversion on annular ring: OSH Park is LOOSER there (5 mil vs PCBWay's 6 mil), so the
# envelope is a genuine per-parameter intersection, not simply "OSH Park's rule sheet". Anyone
# tempted to relax annular to 0.127 because that is what the OSH Park page says would put the
# board out of spec at PCBWay.
#
# Sources: OSH Park docs (two-layer + After Dark service pages, read 2026-07-27) and PCBWay's
# stated 2-layer capability as recorded in this file's previous revision.
#
# SINGLE-TIER HARD FLOORS — the reasoning below is retained because it still governs.
#
# Why a two-tier scheme does not work here:
#   An earlier revision paired an error floor with a looser marginal-band WARNING. That does not
#   do what it reads like. KiCad resolves ONE effective clearance per object pair — the most
#   restrictive applicable min — and reports under the rule that set it. Because the marginal
#   band's value was LOOSER (larger) than the error floor, the band always won: every tight spot
#   came back as a warning and the error floor never fired, masking genuine sub-floor clearances
#   that would have shipped. Two clearance tiers (error < A, warning < B, A < B) cannot coexist.
#   If you want routing margin, put it in the board's net-class clearance (which the router obeys
#   live) — NOT in a second DRU rule. That is a different mechanism and it composes correctly.
#
# Why the gates sit a hair BELOW the fab minimum:
#   These are "do not ship below this" lines, and geometry built exactly at spec must not
#   coin-flip on floating-point rounding. A 0.1524 mm gap computed as 0.15239999 would fail a
#   0.1524 gate for no physical reason. Each floor is therefore set a fraction under the true
#   limit (0.152 vs 0.1524; 0.1499 vs 0.150) — close enough that nothing physically out of spec
#   passes, loose enough that at-spec geometry does not flicker. This is the same trick the
#   annular rule already used, now applied consistently.

(rule "clearance-hard-floor"
  (severity error)
  (constraint clearance (min 0.152mm)))

(rule "track-hard-floor"
  (severity error)
  (constraint track_width (min 0.152mm)))

# Uniform 0.6/0.3 vias sit at exactly 0.150 mm annular by construction, which is EXACTLY
# PCBWay's stated minimum -- zero margin. Left as-is because widening the via pad to 0.65 would
# cost clearance everywhere on a board that is already being re-spaced; flagged in TODO instead.
(rule "annular-hard-floor"
  (severity error)
  (constraint annular_width (min 0.1499mm)))

(rule "drill-floor"
  (severity error)
  (constraint hole_size (min 0.254mm)))

# OSH Park requires 15 mil of copper pullback from the nominal board edge. The GND pours already
# clear it (0.5005 mm measured), and the board's own min_copper_edge_clearance is 0.5 mm, which is
# stricter still. The known and deliberate exception is the pair of 0.25 mm plating-bus stubs that
# cross the outline at x=25.4 to feed electrolytic hard gold at PCBWay -- they are REQUIRED there
# and PROHIBITED at OSH Park, so they are the one object that cannot satisfy both fabs. See
# PCB/README.md: the OSH Park upload deletes those two stubs, and nothing else.
(rule "edge-clearance-floor"
  (severity error)
  (constraint edge_clearance (min 0.381mm)))
